The decisive point from the full text is now clear: the paper proves existence, not an efficient search/classification formula. Its three main results use absorbing and regularity arguments; the cleanest native family is therefore a witness search for a cyclic vertex ordering in an edge-coloured 3-uniform hypergraph, checked by exact edge and colour comparisons. I’m now testing whether a genuinely hard generated distribution can stay in the paper’s Dirac-type regime; a merely sparse planted cycle would be native in syntax but would miss the theorem’s substantive regime.
