1236:Theorem~\ref{tightcycle} demonstrates an interesting contrast between the tight Hamilton cycle problem and the perfect matching problem in $k$-graphs. These two problems share many similarities: both are NP-hard for $k$-graphs in general (see~\cite{GJ74}), and the minimum codegree threshold which ensures the existence of a perfect matching in a $k$-graph $H$ on $n$ vertices (determined asymptotically by K\"uhn and Osthus~\cite{pp-kuhn06} and exactly for large $n$ by R\"odl, Ruci\'nski and Szemer\'edi~\cite{RoRuSz09b}) is close to $n/2$, that is, asymptotically equal to the minimum codegree threshold for a tight Hamilton cycle (see Theorem~\ref{codeg}). 
1239:This complements a previous result of Szyma\'nska~\cite{S}, who showed that for any $\eps > 0$ the problem remains NP-hard when restricted to $k$-graphs $H$ with $\delta(H) \geq n/k-\eps n$. By contrast, we have seen in this paper that the tight Hamilton cycle problem is NP-hard even when restricted to $k$-graphs $H$ with $\delta(H) \geq n/2 - C$ for a constant $C$, \emph{i.e.}, there is a significant difference in the minimum codegree thresholds needed to render each problem tractable.
